cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "feasible-rerank: fair, energy-aware recommendation list selection via QUBO"
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Huzaifa
    family-names: Abdul Rehman
repository-code: "https://github.com/HuzaifaAbdulRehman/feasible-rerank"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - recommender-systems
  - quantum-annealing
  - QUBO
  - fairness
  - green-computing
abstract: >-
  Selects a k-item recommendation list by minimising a QUBO over relevance,
  intra-list diversity and group exposure fairness, with exact-k enforced as a
  penalty term, and reports the energy cost of doing so.

  Five findings are documented. First, the textbook recipe -- penalty-encoded
  cardinality handed to a single-flip simulated annealer -- fails silently at
  realistic candidate-set sizes: two feasible k-sets are never adjacent under
  single-spin-flip moves, so the sampler returns lists that are always the right
  length and close to arbitrary among lists of that length, losing to a greedy MMR
  baseline on its own objective. Tabu search and a constraint-preserving swap
  annealer both recover roughly a factor of two.

  Second, the failure is a property of the encoding rather than of thermal search: a
  continuous-dynamics solver (Simulated Bifurcation) fails on the same problem by a
  different mechanism, and no amount of additional compute rescues either.

  Third, the choice of the diversity and fairness weights decides the QUBO-versus-
  classical comparison by a larger margin than the methods differ from one another; a
  comparison run with the fairness term disabled reverses the conclusion.

  Fourth, under a protocol that tunes every method on a disjoint half of the users, the
  QUBO's advantage is feasibility rather than accuracy: below a group-exposure budget of
  about 0.25 no classical reranker tested can satisfy the constraint at any setting of
  its own hyperparameters. This holds on six benchmarks spanning two domains, two group
  definitions and two retrieval models.

  Fifth, that advantage has a precise boundary: it disappears when the list size divides
  evenly by the number of groups, because a greedy quota allocation is then already
  optimal.
